      text: "Ask any advanced learner what still trips them up, and many will mention idioms. You can know thousands of words and still feel lost when a colleague tells you that a project is \"back to square one\" or that the boss has \"let the cat out of the bag\". We spoke to Helen Ward, a communication coach who works with professionals relocating to the UK, about how to deal with them.\n\n\"The first thing I tell my clients is not to panic,\" Helen says. \"Idioms are the tip of the iceberg of a culture, so of course they feel strange at first. But you don't need to know all of them. Most people use the same thirty or forty again and again.\" She recommends starting with the ones you hear most often at work, such as \"on the same page\", \"cut corners\" or \"go the extra mile\".\n\nThe biggest mistake, according to Helen, is translating idioms word for word. \"One client from Moscow told a meeting that a deadline was 'hanging on a hair'. Everyone understood what he meant, more or less, but it sounded odd. The English version would be 'hanging by a thread'.\" Another common problem is using idioms in the wrong situation. \"Saying that a task is 'a piece of cake' is fine with colleagues, but you wouldn't write it in a report for a client.\"\n\nSo should learners avoid idioms altogether? Helen doesn't think so. \"Using an idiom correctly can really break the ice. People appreciate the effort, and it shows that you understand not just the language but the humour behind it.\" Her advice is to collect idioms in a notebook together with the situation in which you heard them, and to try out one new expression a week.\n\nShe also suggests taking the idiom lists you find online with a pinch of salt. \"Many websites are full of expressions that nobody under eighty has used for decades. If you've never heard a native speaker say 'it's raining cats and dogs', there's probably a reason for that.\"",
