      text: "Andrei had been living in Toronto for six months when his manager took him aside for what she called \"a quick chat\". His work was excellent, she said, but a couple of clients had found his emails a bit abrupt. Andrei was shocked. He had always been polite, he thought. He just didn't see the point of writing three sentences when one would do.\n\nHis experience is extremely common. Linguists use the word \"register\" to describe how formal or informal our language is, and getting it right is one of the hardest parts of learning a foreign language. Grammar books rarely mention it, yet native speakers notice immediately when it's wrong.\n\nInterestingly, learners tend to make mistakes in both directions. In writing, many sound too direct. A sentence like \"Send me the report by Friday\" is perfectly normal in Russian, but in English it can sound like an order. Most native speakers would soften it: \"Could you send me the report by Friday?\" or even \"Would it be possible to have the report by Friday?\" In conversation, on the other hand, the same learners often sound far too formal. Telling a friend \"I would like to inform you that I am unable to attend the party\" is grammatically perfect, but it will probably make them laugh. \"Sorry, I can't make it\" is what people actually say.\n\nSpoken English also has its own little words that hold conversations together: \"anyway\", \"I mean\", \"to be fair\", \"mind you\". They carry almost no meaning on their own, which is exactly why textbooks tend to ignore them, but without them speech sounds robotic. Come to think of it, they work a bit like the Russian \"nu\" or \"v obshchem\".\n\nSo how do you learn to switch registers? Listen carefully to how people speak in different situations, and notice the difference between a podcast and a news bulletin, or between a text message and an official letter. And if a colleague tells you that you sound abrupt, don't take it personally. As Andrei says now, \"At the end of the day, it was the most useful feedback I've ever had.\"",
