      title: "Британский юмор: инструкция",
      text: "Every country laughs, but not every country laughs at the same things. Ask newcomers to Britain what surprised them most, and many will say the humour. \"In my first month I honestly thought my colleagues hated each other,\" says Dmitry, a software engineer who moved from Saint Petersburg to Leeds. \"They were constantly insulting each other. Then I realised that was how they showed they were friends.\"\n\nWhat Dmitry noticed is called banter, and it is at the heart of British social life. Friends take the mickey out of each other's clothes, football teams and cooking, and the person being teased is expected to laugh and hit back. If you get offended, you have broken an unwritten rule. Paradoxically, the more someone teases you, the more they probably like you.\n\nThe second key ingredient is irony. The British often say the opposite of what they mean, usually with a completely straight face. Standing in the pouring rain, someone might remark, \"Lovely weather today.\" This deadpan style can be confusing, because there is no laugh or wink to tell you it's a joke. Sarcasm works in a similar way: \"Oh, brilliant,\" after missing the last train, does not mean that anything is brilliant.\n\nSelf-deprecating humour is the third element. Rather than boasting, people make fun of their own failures. A colleague who has just won an award might say, \"Don't worry, it was a very weak year.\" Learners sometimes take such comments literally and try to reassure the person, which can feel a bit awkward for everyone.\n\nSo how can you join in? Experts recommend listening before speaking. Watch how people react to each other's jokes and pick up the patterns. Start with gentle self-deprecation, which is the safest form, and avoid teasing people you don't know well. And if you completely miss a joke, admit it with a smile. \"I've been here five years,\" Dmitry says, \"and I still crack up when I remember how seriously I took everything at first.\"",
